WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.152
This project combines join reasoning with a window-function idea.

00:00:04.152 --> 00:00:10.487
Inside the CTE, LEAD looks forward within each patient's admissions ordered by date.

00:00:10.487 --> 00:00:17.082
That gives us information about the next admission, including the next department and the next admission date.

00:00:17.082 --> 00:00:23.647
The outer query then keeps cases where a next department exists and where the department changes.

00:00:23.647 --> 00:00:30.032
Finally, it groups the transitions to summarize how often patients move from one department to another.

00:00:30.032 --> 00:00:35.857
Even though the final logic is not a traditional join, the same grain thinking applies.

00:00:35.857 --> 00:00:42.577
Inside the CTE, one row represents one admission plus information about the following admission.

00:00:42.577 --> 00:00:49.292
After grouping, one row represents one transition pair, such as Emergency to Cardiology.

00:00:49.292 --> 00:00:52.657
This type of analysis appears in many fields.

00:00:52.657 --> 00:00:57.722
In retail, you might study transitions from one product category to another.

00:00:57.722 --> 00:01:03.022
In education, you might study students moving from one course level to the next.

00:01:03.022 --> 00:01:06.937
In operations, you might study workflow steps.

00:01:06.937 --> 00:01:11.718
The key habit is to keep restating the grain after every major transformation.
